+| **R8 — failed R2 gate and withhold-scope precedence** | The two-part R2 gate is conjunctive: (1) the creator gives a no-source account; and (2) the editor finds no contrary source-specific evidence and only a commonplace motif. If either part fails or is disputed, R2 is unavailable; that does not prove source use or establish influence. Keep the dated `possible influence: unverified` note and leave the question open; a denial contradicted by a distinctive-sequence finding is a dispute for `l0-questions.md`. Apply R1 only if source use or lineage is independently established. **Precedence:** for a separable affected detail, the more-specific R8 appendix's detail-level rule controls over the parent row's broad "withhold it": withhold the detail, and retain a remainder only if it is independently supportable. Withhold the whole element only when the dependency is inseparable/constitutive or no independently supportable remainder exists. Do not use `external-only / unverified inspiration` unless general-motif use is established with independent in-world support. If both R2 parts later pass, record the basis and remove the provisional note. | A creator's no-source account is evidence, not adjudication; distinctive-sequence evidence does not prove consultation. Attribute a source only if R1 evidence establishes its use or the work is named as a parallel. | Keep open; a failed R2 gate is not a rights assessment. | Keep open; no permission is inferred. | **Owner:** the lore integration owner records the screen and withhold scope; the creator supplies an account; the operator resolves a routed dispute. **Trigger:** either conjunct of the R2 gate fails or remains disputed, especially creator denial plus contrary source-specific sequence evidence. **Worked example:** the creator denies consultation but the editor finds the same seven threshold names in the same order as *Kesh Hymn* lines 12–19. R2 fails, but influence is not proved; retain the note and route the question to `l0-questions.md`. Withhold the sequence detail if the rest of the element is independently supportable; if the seven-stair descent has no separable supportable remainder, withhold the whole element. **Verification command (text completeness only; not an influence finding):** `python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; s=Path(".ufo/waves/round-91/patches/D1-edges.patch").read_text(); a=s.split("**R8 — failed R2 gate",1)[1].split("**R9 — later-reprint boundary",1)[0]; keys=("**Owner:**","**Trigger:**","**Worked example:**","two-part R2 gate","does not prove source use","affected detail","whole element","l0-questions.md","remove the provisional note"); missing=[k for k in keys if k not in a]; print(f"R8 text completeness: {len(keys)-len(missing)}/{len(keys)}"); raise SystemExit(bool(missing))'` |
+| **R9 — later-reprint boundary and tie reliability** | Keep `Delivery mode: translation`; record the actual `Delivered printing:` (edition/year plus stable identifier or copy locator) separately from `Source text/version:`. A later reprint qualifies for the narrow R9 exception only when an auditable, item-specific record identifies the actual delivered reprint and edition/year, gives a stable identifier or copy locator, names the translator and source work, and ties the cited passage to that translation; verify the passage against that exact copy. An inspectable title/imprint/copyright page or publisher/authoritative-library catalogue record can qualify when it supplies those links. A generic re-issue note, untraceable secondary entry, translator name, or anchor alone is insufficient. Record the precise printing and record citation in the excerpt/map. If the item-specific tie or source work is unverified, retain `Source text/version: unidentified; unverified` and fail R5 source-claim support; describe only the checked copy's wording if the reprint itself is what is being reported. Even when the tie qualifies, support only a claim about wording in that identified reprint; do not extend it to an earlier printing's wording or an edition-specific variant unless that exact edition is independently identified and compared. Do not label a delivered translation `not delivered`. | Attribute supported wording only to the identified reprint/translation, not to an earlier printing or the original source wording without separate evidence. | Assess rights for the delivered printing and translation expression; the tie is not a rights conclusion. | Assess separately; a bibliographic tie grants no permission. | **Owner:** the excerpt/map submitter supplies the copy and bibliographic details; the lore integration owner checks the item-specific tie and R5 scope. **Trigger:** an R5 claim relies on a translation from a later reprint or its printing/source version is uncertain. **Worked example:** the JDEP2 excerpt names M. A. Translator and says “2011 reprint of the 1901 translation,” but has only a generic re-issue note and no item-specific record tying the passage to a named source work. Keep `Delivery mode: translation`, retain `Source text/version: unidentified; unverified`, and fail R5 source support. If a qualifying record ties the exact 2011 copy and passage to the translator and named work, support only “the identified 2011 reprint's translation says ...”, not the 1901 printing or a variant unique to another edition. **Verification command (text completeness only; not a source-fidelity finding):** `python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; s=Path(".ufo/waves/round-91/patches/D1-edges.patch").read_text(); a=s.split("**R9 — later-reprint boundary",1)[1].split("**R10 — cluster-local source_id",1)[0]; keys=("**Owner:**","**Trigger:**","**Worked example:**","item-specific record","stable identifier or copy locator","ties the cited passage","that exact copy","identified reprint","earlier printing","edition-specific variant","Source text/version: unidentified; unverified"); missing=[k for k in keys if k not in a]; print(f"R9 text completeness: {len(keys)-len(missing)}/{len(keys)}"); raise SystemExit(bool(missing))'` |
+| **R10 — cluster-local `source_id` namespace** | Each cluster's `sources.json` is a local ID namespace; the comparison key is `(cluster-relative path, source_id)`, not the bare string. A repeated `source_id` across clusters is a namespace collision, not evidence of record identity—even identical row contents do not make IDs global. Every repeated ID triggers manual comparison of the full source rows and an entry in the incoming cluster README under `## R10 comparison outcomes` (both paths, row pointers, compared fields, and why the overlap is dismissed or unresolved). If unresolved source-record identity is material to a claim, also route the question to `l0-questions.md`. Do not place source-record ID collisions in the live register; only incompatible, source-backed claims with a demonstrated shared referent, field, and occasion meet the existing R10 register rule. A per-cluster checker validates only that cluster's local references. | Keep each cluster's in-world source record and claim pointers local; a shared key cannot serve as a cross-cluster witness or real-world attribution. | Rights cannot decide source-record identity or canon. | Training permission cannot decide source-record identity or canon. | **Owner:** the lore integration owner compares and records the outcomes; cluster owners supply the source rows and pointers. **Trigger:** the same literal `source_id` occurs in the `sources.json` files of two landed clusters. **Worked example:** JDEP2's two probes share `registrar-copy` with identical rows and `salt-chronicle` with different titles (municipal registry copy versus courier's route log). Record both overlaps in the incoming README as namespace-local comparisons; neither equality nor difference proves global identity or creates a live-register conflict. **Verification command (comparison receipt only; manual classification remains required; set `CLUSTER_A` and `CLUSTER_B` first):** `python3 corpus/fixtures/check_bible.py "$CLUSTER_A" && python3 corpus/fixtures/check_bible.py "$CLUSTER_B" && python3 -c 'import json,sys;from pathlib import Path;a,b=[Path(p) for p in sys.argv[1:3]];A={r["id"]:r for r in json.loads((a/"sources.json").read_text())};B={r["id"]:r for r in json.loads((b/"sources.json").read_text())};shared=sorted(A.keys()&B.keys());print("shared source_id strings:",shared);[print(k,"A=",A[k],"B=",B[k],"equal=",A[k]==B[k]) for k in shared]' "$CLUSTER_A" "$CLUSTER_B"` |
